cartridge "MARAUDER/C.M.BLAKEMORE/1988"

' MARAUDER
' A ColecoVision game based on "Marauder" by Cleveland M. Blakemore,
' Ahoy! February 1988 (Commodore 64 machine language, 2,145 bytes at
' $C000, started by a two-line BASIC loader; the author is named on the
' issue's contents page: "Marauder for the C-64 by Cleveland M.
' Blakemore"). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic),
' 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/dis.txt), the game is rebuilt for the ColecoVision:
' red cave rock with cracked tiles and sloping walls, a two-colour
' helicopter with a turning rotor, five coloured missiles as smooth
' sprites, a fuel gauge, bursts, sound effects and a title tune.
'
' The game: fly a helicopter through a tunnel that scrolls towards you.
' Five missiles stream across from the right; shoot all five to clear a
' tunnel. Each hit refuels you a little. Touch the rock or a missile and
' you crash; run dry and you fall. Clear the chosen number of tunnels
' (1 to 4: the C64's F1/F3/F5/F7 skill keys) to save the base.
'
' From the original (orig/): the tunnel is a pair of walls one column
' wide, a fixed gap apart, drawn by a walk: the lower wall's row goes down
' below row 12 of 24, up from row 23, otherwise up or down at random, one
' row a column, a peak or valley keeping its row when the walk turns; the
' gap is 11 rows (10 for the hardest skill) and narrows by one row for
' every tunnel cleared, the new width coming in with the new columns;
' missile N (1-5) flies left N pixels a step and comes back in from the
' right when it leaves; the helicopter moves 2 pixels a step in eight
' directions inside a box; one shell at a time, 8 pixels a step, fired
' while the button is held; a hit missile is gone, scores N hundred
' points and gives 3 of the gauge's 52 units of fuel; fuel drops a unit
' every 26 steps; when it runs out the helicopter falls to the bottom and
' crashes; when the five are gone a new five come, one tunnel fewer is
' left (shown as TUNNEL n) and at none the base is saved
' (CONGRATULATIONS!! YOU HAVE SAVED BASE!); touching the rock or a missile
' ends the game; the game waits for the stick before the tunnel moves;
' score and high score (shown as hundreds, BCD).
'
' Liberties: the C64 scrolled a column every pass of its loop (about 48
' a second, 8 pixels each, four times the helicopter's climb: too fast to
' fly); here a column every 4 frames (2 pixels a frame, as fast as the
' helicopter climbs), the missiles 0.5, 0.75, 1, 1.5 and 2 pixels a frame,
' the helicopter 2 pixels a frame as on the C64 and the shell 5, so
' speeds keep the original's order and the game is humanly playable; the
' shell is 3 pixels high and the helicopter's box a little smaller than
' its picture, so a missile that can touch you can mostly be shot; the tunnel is 20 rows high (rows
' 2-21), the walk's limits move to rows 13 and 21 and the gap is 10 rows
' (9 at skill 4), narrowing by one row a tunnel down to 6; a missile that
' comes back in takes a new height inside the tunnel's gap at the right
' edge (on the C64 it kept its height and flew through the rock); the
' walls are solid rock (the C64 drew two lines of slash characters);
' collisions are box tests (the C64 used its sprite collision registers);
' a hit shows a burst where the missile was (the C64 flashed the whole
' screen); the skill is chosen with the stick on the title (1-4 tunnels)
' instead of the function keys; the fuel gauge turns red and beeps when
' low; game end shows a message and goes back to the title (the C64
' restarted at once).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick flies the helicopter, either
' button fires. On the title: up and down choose START, TUNNELS or
' CREDITS; left and right change the number of tunnels; a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.
' Own xorshift random numbers (the test bot replays them).
'
' Workarounds for Amy bugs 25 (sprite count) removed 2026-11-01.

const TRk0 = 128
const TRk1 = 129
const TTd = 130
const TTu = 131
const TBd = 132
const TBu = 133
const TG0 = 134
const TG1 = 135
const TG2 = 136
const TG3 = 137
const TG4 = 138
const TH0 = 139
const TH1 = 140
const TH2 = 141
const TH3 = 142
const TH4 = 143
const TQ0 = 144
const TQ1 = 145
const TQ2 = 146
const TQ3 = 147
const TQ4 = 148
const TQ5 = 149
const TQ6 = 150
const TQ7 = 151
const TQ8 = 152
const TQ9 = 153
const TQ10 = 154
const TQ11 = 155
const TQ12 = 156
const TQ13 = 157
const TQ14 = 158
const TQ15 = 159
const SHb = 0
const SHc0 = 4
const SHc1 = 8
const SMs = 12
const SSh = 16
const SEx0 = 20
const SEx1 = 24
const NTiles8 = 256
const SprBytes = 224
const SndTuneA = 1
const SndTuneB = 2
const SndZap = 3
const SndHit = 4
const SndBurst = 5
const SndCrash = 6
const SndLow = 7
const SndClear = 8
const SndStart = 9
const SndWon = 10
const SndOver = 11
const NSpr = 9
const ScPer = 4
const FuelMax = 52
const FuelPer = 32
const TopRow = 2
const MidRow = 12

